1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board-size ambiguity dominates the round. With the usual infinite/19×19 interpretation the D1–D2 white two-stone group has the three liberties E1 E2 D3, so White is not in immediate self-atari. That is the interpretation used by Colleagues 1,2,4,5,6,8.

Under that interpretation Black has three essentially linked groups

* C1–C2 : {B1,B2}
* A2     : {A1,A3,B2}
* B3–B4  : {A3,A4,B2}

and the only points that touch the net are A1 A3 A4 B1 B2.  
B2 is the central shared liberty.  W B1 puts C1–C2 in atari on B2 and W B2 is the only liberty of a W B2 stone, so the two moves are mutual atari.  After

1.W B1 2.B B2

the A2/C1–C2/B3–B4 stones merge to a six-stone group with the four liberties A1 A3 A4 B1 and the white stone on B1 is itself on A1. Black can capture the attacking stone with A1 and the merged group keeps ≥2 liberties.

1.W A4 is the move advocated by Colleague 1.  After 1.W A4 the B3–B4 group is reduced to {A3,B2}.  The lines quoted

* 2.B A3 3.W B2 – Black can now play B1 capturing W B2 and re-opening B2 for the merged group;
* 2.B B2 3.W B1 – Black can capture W B1 with A1;
* 2.B A1 3.W B2 – Black can again capture W B2 with B1;
* 2.B B1 3.W B2 – W B2 is legal and captures C1–C2 but the A2/B3–B4 complex is left with A1 A3 and Black forces A3.

In every branch Black has a recapture/defence that restores at least two liberties to the corner formation and removes the attacking white stone, so A4 is not a forced kill. The same snap-back appears for B1, B2, A3, A1 etc. The mutual atari at B1/B2 and the ability of Black to capture the attacking stone at A1/B1 makes a forced capture impossible. That is the consensus of Colleagues 2,4,5,6,8, all of whom report {}.

Colleague 3’s {B1} kill depends on treating B2 as a self-atari after W B1, i.e. on the group-liberty miscount that B2 would have no liberties at all after W B1. In standard Go the liberties of a group are counted for the whole group, so B2 remains legal for Black and the C1–C2 group is saved by connection.

Colleague 7’s {D3} argument is the small-board interpretation where columns are A–D and rows 1–5, so E1/E2 are off board and the D1–D2 white group really has the single liberty D3. On that 4×5 board 1.W D3 is forced to save the own group, and the subsequent corner net is still not forced – Black can still play B2, A3, A1, etc., and recapture the attacking stones as above. The move therefore saves the own group but does not give a forced kill.

Overall the deliberation shows no first move that guarantees a capture against best Black defence under the normal board interpretation; the majority view is the empty set. The main unresolved point is the board-edge assumption: on a 4×5 board D3 is mandatory for survival, but it is not a forced killing move.

