4.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board is the usual infinite /19×19 interpretation so the White D1-D2 group has E1,E2,D3 and White is not forced to save it. The only material at stake is the Black corner

* C1-C2 {B1,B2}
* A2 {A1,A3,B2}
* B3-B4 {A3,A4,B2}

B2 is the common liberty and B1/B2 are a mutual atari.

After 1.W B1 the C1-C2 pair is on B2 and W B1 is on A1,B2. B B2 is legal because the stone joins A2,B3,B4,C1,C2 into a group with liberties A1,A3,A4; it does not self-atari, it merely puts W B1 on the single liberty A1. B A1 is also a legal defense and puts W B1 in atari on B2. Hence B1 is not a forced kill; the claim that B2 is illegal after W B1 is wrong.

W B2 is an immediate snap-back: W B2 has the sole liberty B1 and B B1 captures it.

W A1 is taken at once by B B1, W A3 is taken at once by B A4. W A4 is the classic net point but it is not forcing on an infinite board. After 1.W A4, W A4 {A3,A5}. Black can play 2.B A5. W A4 still has A3 but A3 is suicide for White after A5 is on the board because W A3 would be surrounded by A2,B3,A4. White cannot save A4 and on the next move B A3 captures the A4 stone. The same A5 defence also defeats the more elaborate A4 case analysis: after 1.W A4 Black can also answer B B1, B B2, B A3, B A1 etc and keep ≥2 liberties on the merged A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 formation, or capture the probing stone on A1/B1/A4. The A5/A3 net that Colleagues 8,5,6 pointed out is decisive.

D3 is only mandatory on a 4-column artificial edge; on a normal board it is a non-forcing defensive save and does not create a zugzwang on the corner.

Hence after correcting the self-atari mis-evaluation of B2 after W B1, and taking into account the A5 defence to A4, no first White point puts Black in zugzwang and forces a capture of A2,B3,B4,C1,C2. The current best assessment is the empty set. The earlier belief in a unique {A4} first move is now regarded as unsupported because Black can answer A5 and capture the net. The B1 claim of Colleague 7 is invalidated by the legality of B B2 merging. Unresolved is whether any exotic outside move can create a later forced capture, but for a first-move forced kill the evidence favours {}.

